21:- **Research notes** (2026-11-15): the Anthropic 2026 agentic coding trends report (read in full; a vendor document with no methodology, customer-reported figures) and OpenHands (documentation, not source) support three priorities here: VR-1 and VR-2 (the report's own evidence is that people delegate what is cheaply checkable, so verifiability is the delegation boundary), PA-1 (it names a view of concurrent agent sessions) and AC-1 (above). They are framing, not evidence about Nexus.
52:| AC-1 | ACP adapter over the sidecar protocol (stdio), hand-rolled JSON-RPC with no dependency: `initialize` with a capability list, `session/new`, `session/load`, `session/prompt`, `session/cancel`; streaming `tool_call` and `tool_call_update` with diff content; a `verification` update carrying receipt ids and verifier results; client-bound `session/request_permission` with allow-once and reject only (no "allow always" at first); one extension, `_nexus/receipt/get`. Reference: goose, Cline, opencode, Gemini CLI ACP servers and the Codex app-server (all four ACP servers use the same three-option permission request; opencode attaches the diff to the request so the user decides with the change in view). The host side exists too: OpenHands' Agent Canvas drives Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI and OpenCode over ACP (docs, 2026-11-15), so an ACP server is what makes Nexus drivable by such hosts | a scripted ACP client completes a task; an `allow` reply to an action the gates deny has no effect (gate test G-3); the receipt is retrievable | L | P1 |
61:| VR-1 | `nexus verify` (no agent run): given a worktree or a base and head, run the discovered or configured verifier, the type-check evidence (CG-4), the verification-file guard and the independent reviewer, and print a verdict VERIFIED / REGRESSION_FREE / UNVERIFIED / BLOCKED with the receipt; exit code per the documented contract; usable as a CI gate (pairs with the v1.2.1 GitHub Action, RD-4) | a known-bad diff (weakened test) is not VERIFIED; the same diff produced by another harness gives the same verdict | M | P1 |
70:| BG-3 | Event triggers (file change, webhook) | deferred: needs a trust and abuse model first | M | P2 (v1.4) |
101:Phase A (foundation, parallelisable): PF-1, PF-2, GV-4, VR-1, GV-3. These unblock the rest and cost little.
104:Phase D (stretch, may roll to v1.4): PA-3, HK-3, BG-3.
107:Dependencies on earlier plans: v1.2.1 RD-3 (`stream-json` output) feeds PF-2; RD-4 (GitHub Action) pairs with VR-1; CS-9 (per-command approval rules, v1.2.2) and GV-1 must agree on rule semantics (allow < prompt < forbidden, deciding rule recorded); MT-1 (the `agent.ts` split) makes AC-1 and BG-1 much easier and should land first if possible; PR-* (provider registry) is independent; MQ-* (measurement) should publish at least one result before this release claims anything.
115:| G-3 | **Gate-bypass suite:** a hostile client, hook, plugin, schedule or policy file cannot approve a denied action, raise a verdict, widen a policy tier, or remove a verification-file protection |
118:| G-6 | Docs and examples for every new surface (`docs/PROTOCOL.md`, hooks, plugins, policy, schedules, audit) with runnable examples in CI |
139:| A new socket or listener widens the attack surface | unix socket mode 0600 by default; token and origin check for any HTTP listener; G-3 |
140:| A client answers approvals in a way that bypasses a gate | the gate decides first; the client only supplies a human's yes or no; G-3 tests it |
// Generates the JSON Schema (draft 2020-12) of the document `nexus verify --json` prints (the same document
// `--receipt <file>` writes), from the type `VerifyJsonOutput` in src/verify_contract.ts, with the `SchemaBuilder` of
// gen_protocol_schema.mjs (see docs/protocol/README.md): deterministic output, compared byte for byte.
//
//   node scripts/gen_verify_result_schema.mjs           write docs/protocol/verify-result-v<N>.schema.json
//   node scripts/gen_verify_result_schema.mjs --check   exit 1 when the checked-in schema or the version ledger is stale
import path from "node:path";
import { fileURLToPath } from "node:url";
import { generateResultSchema, repo, resultLedgerProblem, resultSchemaMain } from "./result_schema.mjs";

const SCRIPT = "gen_verify_result_schema";

export const VERIFY_RESULT_LEDGER_FILE = path.join(repo, "docs", "protocol", "VERIFY_RESULT_VERSIONS.json");
export const verifyResultSchemaFile = (version) => path.join(repo, "docs", "protocol", `verify-result-v${version}.schema.json`);

/** The schema text for `VerifyJsonOutput` as src/verify_contract.ts declares it now, and its `VERIFY_RESULT_SCHEMA_VERSION`. */
export function generateVerifyResultSchema() {
  return generateResultSchema({
    script: SCRIPT,
    source: "src/verify_contract.ts",
    rootType: "VerifyJsonOutput",
    versionConst: "VERIFY_RESULT_SCHEMA_VERSION",
    title: (version) => `nexus verify --json output v${version}`,
    description:
      "Generated from VerifyJsonOutput in cli/src/verify_contract.ts by cli/scripts/gen_verify_result_schema.mjs. Do not edit by hand. The one JSON document `nexus verify --json` writes to stdout and `--receipt` writes to a file; `schema_version` is its first member.",
  });
}

